# This is the Wic partition-layout template for the Yocto SD-card image. The
# Yocto preparation script replaces the two size placeholders during
# make yocto-prepare and writes the completed file to the build configuration
# directory.
# BitBake then asks Wic to create one disk image containing the three
# partitions below.
#
# part is the short form of the Wic partition command. --ondisk mmcblk0 names
# the disk as it will appear on the Raspberry Pi. It does not read or modify
# /dev/mmcblk0 on the computer running the build.
#
# Sizes without a unit are in MiB. One MiB is 1,048,576 bytes. --fixed-size
# requests an exact partition size. Wic stops the build if the files assigned
# to a partition do not fit in that size.
#
# Wic part command reference:
# https://docs.yoctoproject.org/scarthgap/ref-manual/kickstart.html#command-part-or-partition

# Partition 1: Raspberry Pi boot files
#
# /boot makes this partition available at /boot and lets Wic add the matching
# mount information to the generated system.
# --source bootimg-partition copies the firmware, configuration, device trees,
# and kernel selected by the Yocto image configuration.
# --fstype=vfat creates a FAT filesystem that Raspberry Pi firmware can read.
# --label boot gives the filesystem a readable name.
# --active sets the partition-table boot flag.
# --align 4096 starts the partition on a 4096 KiB, or 4 MiB, boundary.
# --fixed-size 100 makes the boot partition exactly 100 MiB.
part /boot --source bootimg-partition --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=vfat --label boot --active --align 4096 --fixed-size 100

# Partition 2: Linux root filesystem
#
# / makes this the filesystem mounted as the Linux root directory.
# --source rootfs fills it with the complete root filesystem built by Yocto.
# --fstype=ext4 creates an ext4 filesystem.
# --label root gives the filesystem the label root.
# --align 4096 uses the same 4 MiB alignment as the boot partition.
# The preparation script replaces ROOT_PARTITION_SIZE_MIB below with the root
# size from storage_layout.conf.
part / --source rootfs --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label root --align 4096 --fixed-size @ROOT_PARTITION_SIZE_MIB@

# Partition 3: persistent IoT App data
#
# There is no mount point after part and no --source option. Wic therefore
# creates an empty ext4 partition without adding it to /etc/fstab.
# --label iot-data lets the first-boot storage program verify that it found the
# correct partition before changing its size.
# The preparation script replaces DATA_PARTITION_BOOTSTRAP_SIZE_MIB with the
# small initial size, currently 64 MiB. iot-app-storage.service runs before IoT
# App, expands this partition to the remaining SD-card space, and mounts it at
# /data. Leaving out the mount point here prevents the partition from being
# mounted before that expansion is complete.
part --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label iot-data --align 4096 --fixed-size @DATA_PARTITION_BOOTSTRAP_SIZE_MIB@
